llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
127; changes code that patch 101 adds): on gfx803, flash-attention tiles
that serve one query head per K/V head run at most 32 columns wide, with a
register budget chosen per head size.
gfx803-specific: engages only for cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4 (host) and GCN4
(device code).

WHY
---
Image prompts are slow on gfx803. With Qwen3.5 9B and its vision projector,
a 1920x1080 image (2061 prompt tokens) takes 50.4 s to process against 8.8 s
for 2028 text tokens. rocprof puts 38.7 s of it in one kernel, the vision
encoder's flash attention: 27 calls of flash_attn_tile<72, 72, 64, 1>, 1.43
s each for about 307 GFLOP (8160 patches attending to 8160, 16 heads), or
0.21 TFLOPS.

A vision encoder gives each query head its own K and V, so its attention
runs the tile kernel with ncols2 == 1. Patch 101 moved the wide tiles with
ncols2 > 1 to the fp32 path; the ncols2 == 1 tiles stay on the half2 path,
because on the fp32 path they spill more and run slower. For prompt-sized
batches they run 64 columns wide, and at the AMD table's occupancy of 2
(128 VGPRs) the 64x1 tile spills for every head size: 154 VGPRs to scratch
at head size 40, 305 at 72, 578 at 80, 817 at 112, and 568 at 128 even at
patch 101's occupancy of 1. Text models whose GQA ratio is odd run the same
tiles: Qwen2.5-Coder 7B (head size 128, GQA 7) processes a 512-token prompt
8192 tokens deep at 60 tok/s.

Narrower tiles need fewer registers. Raising the 64x1 tile to occupancy 1
only halves the time (head size 72: 1.43 -> 0.72 s), and moving these tiles
to the fp32 path makes them 2.2x slower (head size 72). The 32- and
16-column tiles, at occupancy 1 where it helps, spill little or not at all
and run 1.9-5.7x faster.

WHAT
----
launch_fattn_tile_switch_ncols1() never picks the 64-column tile for
ncols2 == 1 on gfx803, and picks the 32-column tile only for head sizes
40, 64, 80 and 96 (ggml_cuda_fattn_tile_gcn4_ncols2_1_max_ncols()); other
head sizes use at most 16 columns. Device code looks up a tile's
configuration with its ncols2 (the device getters take it as a template
argument); on GCN4 with ncols2 == 1 a new table,
ggml_cuda_fattn_tile_get_config_amd_gcn4_ncols2_1(), gives the 16- and
32-column tiles occupancy 1 for head sizes 64, 80, 96, 112, 128 and 256 and
falls back to the gcn4 table otherwise. Only occupancy changes, so the host,
which reads the AMD table, still computes the right thread counts and batch
sizes. Tiles with ncols2 > 1 compile to the same code as before (VGPR,
occupancy and spill counts of every instance compared).

Measured on an RX 590, test-backend-ops perf, 16 heads, f16 K/V, no mask,
ms per call at 1024 x 1024 / 8160 x 8160:

    head size  40:    8.4 /  522.3  ->   2.4 /  138.8
               64:   15.4 /  957.4  ->   3.3 /  168.4
               72:   22.0 / 1389.8  ->   4.4 /  254.2
               80:   29.5 / 1820.7  ->   5.6 /  327.2
               96:   39.9 / 2495.7  ->   8.0 /  434.2
              112:   43.7 / 2727.2  ->  10.0 /  681.1
              128:   29.3 / 1904.3  ->   9.2 /  535.1
              256:  108.2 / 6818.8  ->  53.8 / 3506.4

The 1920x1080 image with Qwen3.5 9B (llama-server, flash attention on,
q8_0 KV cache): 50.4 -> 18.7 s; the 2028-token text prompt is unchanged
(8.77 s, the model's text layers use GQA). The same image with Qwen3.6
35B-A3B Q4_K_M and its vision projector, every expert in system RAM
(-ncmoe 99, 1024-token batches): 47.3 -> 16.0 s, generation unchanged
(23.3 / 23.9 tok/s). Qwen2.5-Coder 7B Q4_K_M,
llama-bench -fa 1 -ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0 -p 512 at depth 0 / 4096 / 8192:
208.0 / 93.8 / 60.2 -> 224.8 / 158.8 / 123.1 tok/s.

The results are not bit-identical: a different tile width sums in a
different order. Against the CPU backend (1024 x 1024, 16 heads, with and
without mask), the normalized mean squared error is the same as before for
head sizes 40-96 (2.5e-5 - 3.1e-5) and lower for 112, 128 and 256 (2.5e-5 -
2.9e-5 -> 3.4e-6 - 5.2e-6).

Verified on an RX 590: test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT
passes against the CPU backend (3982/3982, 1913 of them with ncols2 == 1).
To reproduce the slowdown, run test-backend-ops perf -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT with
a case of head size 72, 16 heads, one query head per K/V head, 4096 queries
and keys, f16 K/V and no mask, with and without the patch.

